    "body": "The HTTP server frames a request body by Content-Length when its `Transfer-Encoding` is not exactly `chunked`.\n\n`LoadBody()` reads only the first `Transfer-Encoding` header. With `gzip`, `chunked, gzip`, or `identity` followed by a second `chunked` header, the body is read by Content-Length, or not at all. RFC 9112 section 6.3 requires a 400 when chunked is not the final coding. The server supports no other coding. I now accept only a single `chunked`, across all `Transfer-Encoding` headers, and answer anything else with a 400. Unlike #36243, I still allow `Content-Length` next to `chunked`, as the existing `interface_http.py` test expects. The new rejection cases in `httpserver_tests` and `interface_http.py` fail on master and pass with this change. The framing check in the `http_request` fuzz target needed the same change, or it fails on `Transfer-Encoding: chunked, `.\n",
